WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.600
The quest for gold is long, storied, and bloody.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.470
So much of the human story--the expansion
of empires, the destruction of entire cultures

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:14.440
--has hinged on the desire to get more of
this mysterious yellow metal.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.439
But that’s actually kinda weird when you
think about it.

00:00:17.439 --> 00:00:23.609
Unlike most assets, gold doesn’t offer any
real-world value like, say, a business or

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:25.289
a handful of grain.

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:29.420
As Warren Buffet puts it, “It doesn’t
do anything but sit there and look at you.”

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:36.140
John Maynard Keyens even referred to gold's connection to currency as a “barbarous relic”...so if that’s true,

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:41.230
why am I hearing constant ads from gold brokers
on the radio almost 100 years later?

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:47.620
Should we be making space for it in our portfolios
or are we being sold a bunch of fools gold?

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.480
Gold and humanity go way way back.

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:06.190
From Egyptian funerary masks to Celtic ruins,
Peruvian temples to ancient Chinese tombs,

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:10.790
evidence of our love of gold is found in practically
every corner of the globe.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:16.049
Throughout this long history, it’s main
purpose has always been for decoration.

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:20.930
Even today most of the gold consumed each
year is for jewelry which makes sense because

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.660
gold is well...“SHINY!”

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.259
That beautiful golden
hue is thanks to the fact that it absorbs

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:32.759
more blue light than any other visible wavelength
of light and since blue is complementary to

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:35.159
yellow, that’s what we see.

00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:39.400
Marketers have long taken advantage of the
fact that yellow is the color we associate

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.750
with happiness, brightness and possibility.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:46.299
It also has some important industrial uses.

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:50.550
Gold reflects most of the solar radiation
in space which is why you can find it in the

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.200
helmets of astronauts and their equiment.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.110
It has a variety of medical uses.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:59.649
And trace amounts of gold are found in millions
of cell phones around the world.

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:05.310
But industrial use is one of the smallest
pieces of the gold consumption pie hovering

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.200
around 10%.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.530
After jewelry, its main use is as a financial
tool, because it seems more reliable than

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:17.540
wildly fluctuating stock markets, or fiat
currencies that can be devalued on the whims

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:23.140
of the government. Gold is traditionally accepted
as a popular hedge against a crisis - and

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:27.500
you can often see the price jump when fears
are running high.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.970
The Great Recession drove gold prices to all-time
highs over a few short years.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:37.790
And since the COVID19 outbreak began, the
price of gold has been steadily climbing.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:42.080
Despite the fact that it doesn’t pay any
dividends and can’t be usefully consumed

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.900
like, say, oil or timber, gold can be a profitable
investment.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:52.450
National emergencies like the one we’re
in right now can make self-described gold-bugs

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:54.260
look really smart all of a sudden.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:03:00.310
Over the last 15 years, gold has increased
in value 278% -- compared to the Dow Jones

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:04.260
Industrial Average which grew only 173%..

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:10.910
This is why gold-brokers run ads framing gold
as a safe, historic investment for the long-term,

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:14.780
while stoking fears of out-of-control governments
and societal collapse.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:19.500
But is it the best long-term choice for growing
your wealth?

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:20.540
Probably not.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.960
If we expanded the amount of time we looked
back to 30 years instead of 15, gold’s growth

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.060
is still hovering right at the 280% mark.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:34.930
The DJI on the other hand has posted 839%
growth since 1990.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.620
But what if we compared it to something else
like bonds?

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:43.540
Well, going back to the 1920’s investment-grade
corporate bonds have had an average annual

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:47.160
rate of return of around 5.25%.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.950
That would mean in that same 30 year time-frame
even investment grade bonds would have outperformed

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.820
gold two to one.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:59.320
Many investors frame gold as a perfect hedge
for inflation.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:04.090
This is because even though the supply of
US dollars goes up, the amount of gold remains

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:05.760
relatively the same.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.900
But that argument is sometimes lacking the
context of timeframe.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:15.180
If expressed in gold, the salary of a Roman
soldier is really similar to the military pay

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.750
in the US today.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:22.840
But in the late 1980’s when inflation went
from 1% to an eye-watering 6%, gold prices

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:24.280
sagged.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:29.479
Goes to show you that time-frame is really
important when evaluating the appropriateness

00:04:29.479 --> 00:04:30.800
of an investment.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.460
Alright, but what about safety?

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.380
Surely gold isn’t as wild and chaotic as
the stock market, that’s the whole appeal, right?

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.680
Uh, wrong.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:43.500
As a matter of fact, there’s a strong case
to be made that gold can be much MORE risky

00:04:43.509 --> 00:04:44.810
than the stock market.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:46.270
That’s because of volatility.

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:48.759
Think of it like waves on the ocean.

00:04:48.759 --> 00:04:52.469
A stormy sea has high volatility with big
waves and toughs.

00:04:52.469 --> 00:04:55.910
A calm sea is less volatile.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:05:00.789
Standard deviation is one way we measure the
amount of volatility that an investment has

00:05:00.789 --> 00:05:02.851
experienced in the past.

00:05:02.851 --> 00:05:05.889
The higher the standard deviation, the riskier
the investment.

00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:11.780
And since 1975, gold’s standard deviation
has actually been about 50 percent greater

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:12.780
than stocks!

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:17.639
So we’re talking about an investment that
over the long haul performs worse than stocks

00:05:17.639 --> 00:05:19.189
but has higher risk.

00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:21.069
Am I missing something here?

00:05:21.069 --> 00:05:22.139
Kind of.

00:05:22.139 --> 00:05:26.870
Pairing assets that move differently from
one another is key to having a diversified

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:28.169
portfolio.

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:32.930
Every type of investment-- be it a stock,
bond, piece of real estate or bar of gold

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:37.490
comes with itsown unique set of risk-factors
that can affect its value.

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:42.439
So by having different types of investments,
you can effectively reduce your overall exposure

00:05:42.439 --> 00:05:43.449
to risk.

00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:47.940
The fact that its value doesn’t directly
correlate with the movement of stocks, bonds

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:52.159
or real estate makes it a unique flavor in
the investing world.

00:05:52.159 --> 00:05:56.410
We should also point out that there are MANY
ways of investing in gold.You can buy it in

00:05:56.410 --> 00:06:01.150
the form of coins or bullion, but dealers
have markups and then there’s the cost of

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:05.039
having to store it safely to protect it from
theft or loss.

00:06:05.039 --> 00:06:09.660
Many gold investors opt for ETFs that have
gold as their primary asset.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:13.310
You can even find mutual funds that own shares
in mining companies.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:17.419
Just know that you’re probably going to
be exposed to the risk of other precious metals

00:06:17.419 --> 00:06:19.650
like silver or copper if you go those routes.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:24.280
If you’ve been following us for a while
you know that seemingly simple questions like

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:25.990
“should I invest in gold?”

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:28.349
do not have simple answers.

00:06:28.349 --> 00:06:33.419
Dismissing it as a hair-brained fixture of
doomsday preppers isn’t entirely fair, but

00:06:33.419 --> 00:06:38.330
neither is it the slam-dunk inflation-hedge
that gold bugs make it out to be.

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:43.979
The mystery and allure surrounding gold is
part of what drives our need for it.

00:06:43.979 --> 00:06:49.520
It may not be a great builder of wealth, but
its ability to preserve and maintain value

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:52.960
over the centuries has made it a remarkable tool.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:56.940
So keeping your eye on the facts instead of the hype, will keep you from getting

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:59.020
caught up in the gold-rush.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:00.940
And that’s our two cents!

